  {
   "title": "Two hows, two wheres, and whose",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English uses one word for *how* and one for *where*. Tagalog has two of each, and the choice depends on the kind of answer you expect."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Use it when…",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[gaáno|other]]  (how)",
       "the answer expresses a **degree or quality** (how large, how far…)",
       "Gaáno kalakí ang Pilipinas?"
      ],
      [
       "[[paáno|other]]  (how)",
       "the answer **details a process** (the way something is done)",
       "Paáno mo malaláman?"
      ],
      [
       "[[nasaán|other]]  (where)",
       "you ask for the **location of a person or thing**",
       "Nasaán siyá?"
      ],
      [
       "[[saán|other]]  (where)",
       "you ask for the **location of an action**",
       "Saán kayó nakatirá?"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Choosing the right word"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Gaáno kalakí ang Pilipinas?",
       "en": "How large is the Philippines?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Gaáno",
         "how (degree)",
         "other"
        ],
