  {
   "title": "Plural question words",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Lesson 4 you learned the plurality rule: the predicate and the subject show when more than one is meant, with plural markers such as **siná, niná, ng mga** and **ang mga**. Question words follow the plurality rule too."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The plural form of a question word is made by **doubling** the whole word or some of its syllables. Use it when more than one thing is being referred to and you expect an answer in the plural."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Rule",
      "Examples"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "1. A word of **two syllables**: double the whole word.",
       "alín → **alín-alín**;  síno → **sínu-síno**"
      ],
      [
       "2. A word of **three or more syllables**: double only the first two syllables.",
       "kaníno → **kaní-kanino**;  magkano → **magka-magkano**"
      ],
      [
       "3. The **o** in the last syllable becomes **u** in the first half of the doubled word.",
       "anó → **anu-anó**;  síno → **sinu-síno**"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "How to double a question word",
     "note": "The book prints these doubled forms with slightly different accents in different places, for example sínu-síno and sinu-síno."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
