  {
   "title": "Short forms: 'yan and 'to",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Just as in English, spoken Tagalog often shortens a word, or runs two words together. With question words, a **ligature** (Lesson 7) can give another version of the same question."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Iyán** and **itó** can also be shortened to **'yan** and **'to**. They lose the stress on their second syllable, because each becomes a one-syllable word."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Full",
      "With a ligature",
      "Short",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Síno ang táong iyán?",
       "Sínong táo iyán?",
       "Síno 'yan?",
       "Who is that person? / Who is that (person)?"
      ],
      [
       "Kaníno ang aklát na itó?",
